    "question": {
      "pdfName": "Engineering_mathematics_2.pdf",
      "text": "For \\(x\\ne0\\), let\n\\[\nf(x)=\\frac{e^{1/x}}{1-e^{1/x}},\n\\]\nand \\(f(0)=0\\). Then \\(f(x)\\) is",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "continuous at \\(x=0\\)."
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "not continuous at \\(x=0\\)."
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "continuous everywhere."
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "none of these."
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "B",
      "correctText": "not continuous at \\(x=0\\).",
      "explanation": "As \\(x\\to0^-\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to0^-}\\frac{e^{1/x}}{1-e^{1/x}}=\\frac{0}{1-0}=0.\n\\]\nAs \\(x\\to0^+\\),\n\\[\n\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\frac{e^{1/x}}{1-e^{1/x}}\n=\\lim_{x\\to0^+}\\frac{1}{e^{-1/x}-1}=-1.\n\\]\nSince the left- and right-hand limits are unequal, \\(f(x)\\) is not continuous at \\(x=0\\). Choice (B).",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
    "classification": {
      "subject": "Engineering Mathematics",
