    "question": {
      "pdfName": "Engineering_mathematics_2.pdf",
      "text": "If \\(xy=6\\), then find the minimum value of \\(2x+3y\\), \\(x,y\\in\\mathbb{R}^+\\).",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "12"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "15"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "9"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "6"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "A",
      "correctText": "12",
      "explanation": "Given \\(xy=6\\), \\(y=6/x\\). Therefore\n\\[\n2x+3y=2x+\\frac{18}{x}.\n\\]\nLet \\(f(x)=2x+18/x\\). Then\n\\[\nf'(x)=2-\\frac{18}{x^2},\\qquad f''(x)=\\frac{36}{x^3}>0.\n\\]\nFor a maximum or minimum, \\(f'(x)=0\\):\n\\[\n2-\\frac{18}{x^2}=0\\Rightarrow x=3.\n\\]\nThen \\(y=6/x=2\\). Hence the minimum value is\n\\[\n2(3)+3(2)=12.\n\\]\nChoice (A).",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
