    "question": {
      "pdfName": "Engineering_mathematics_2.pdf",
      "text": "Find the maximum value of the function \\(f(x)=3x^4-2x^3-6x^2+6x+1\\) in the interval \\([-1,2]\\).",
      "type": "MCQ",
      "options": [
        {
          "label": "A",
          "text": "$\\frac{39}{16}$"
        },
        {
          "label": "B",
          "text": "2"
        },
        {
          "label": "C",
          "text": "21"
        },
        {
          "label": "D",
          "text": "18"
        }
      ],
      "marks": null,
      "negativeMarks": null,
      "difficulty": null
    },
    "answer": {
      "correctOption": "C",
      "correctText": "21",
      "explanation": "Let\n\\[\nf(x)=3x^4-2x^3-6x^2+6x+1.\n\\]\nThen\n\\[\nf'(x)=12x^3-6x^2-12x+6.\n\\]\nSetting \\(f'(x)=0\\),\n\\[\n2x^3-x^2-2x+1=0=(x-1)(x+1)(2x-1).\n\\]\nThus \\(x=\\pm1,\\frac12\\). Evaluate at the critical points and endpoints:\n\\[\n\\max\\left\\{f(-1),f\\left(\\frac12\\right),f(1),f(2)\\right\\}\n=\\max\\left\\{-6,\\frac{39}{16},2,21\\right\\}=21.\n\\]\nChoice (C).",
      "status": "confirmed"
    },
    "classification": {
      "subject": "Engineering Mathematics",
